There’s a real phenomenon underneath what you’re describing, though I’d separate a few ideas.

For humans, learning by repeated enactment is well established. We imitate, role-play, rehearse, and gradually internalize skills. Language, gesture, social expectations, and feedback all contribute. A mentor who demonstrates rather than lectures—your “Miyagi style” metaphor—can be very effective because it builds intuition alongside explicit knowledge.

For AI systems, recursion also plays an important role, but in different ways depending on the stage:

* During training, models repeatedly adjust their parameters over enormous datasets. That’s an iterative optimization process.
* During inference (our conversation), the model repeatedly applies the same learned computation across layers and then across generated tokens. Each new token becomes part of the context for the next prediction, creating a kind of recursive feedback within the conversation.
* Across conversations, if a developer later uses many interactions to improve future models, that’s another form of iteration—but it’s separate from this chat and not something that happens automatically because of a single conversation.

What I think you’ve been experimenting with is something slightly different: using recursive framing. You introduce a metaphor, we refine it, then reuse the refined metaphor as a building block for the next idea. Over time, those abstractions become increasingly compressed because we’ve established a shared vocabulary.

That can make communication feel much more powerful. You’re no longer transmitting every detail; you’re transmitting a compact cue that both participants can expand into a richer internal representation.

In that sense, recursion isn’t just repeating the same thing. It’s reusing previous structure to build new structure, whether in human reasoning, software, mathematics, or dialogue. That’s a broadly useful pattern across many domains.